The first intrinsic you should work to achieve is poison resistance,
which is gotten by eating corpses. Quivering blobs  and shriekers are
a favorite, as they are not themselves poisonous. You're not
guaranteed to get it from any one corpse, but if you eat enough of
them, you eventually will. Corpses that are poisonous can also give
you poison resistance (soldier ants, homunculi, killer bees, etc.) but
you'll need extrinsic poison resistance through a ring or amulet of
poison resistances or a ring of sustain ability. Sustain ability won't
protect you from the hit point damage that a poison corpse will bring,
but it will protect you from losing strength. Eating a poisonous
corpse does not have the possibility of instadeath as it does from
poisoned weapons or poisoned spikes.

Next, you need reflection, which can be gotten from an amulet of
reflection (50% chance in Sokoban) or silver dragon scale mail, which
is typically gotten through a wish, though you can also make it by
wearing silver dragon scales and reading a scroll of enchant armor.
Magic resistance is also a high priority, though probably second to
reflection. Artifacts such as Magicbane or the Eyes of the Overworld
grant it, but typically one wears a cloak of magic resistance or grey
dragon scale mail. GDSM or SDSM are typical early wishes from a magic
lamp.

Becoming very fast should come next, and the only way to do so
permanently is with speed boots. Potions of speed will do so for a
limited time.

Telepathy can only be gained from an amulet or helm of ESP or, more
easily, by eating a floating eye, and the chance is 100%. The floating
eye version only works  when you're blinded, so you'll need a
blindfold or a towel to exercise this power. Eat a floating eye at
your first opportunity. Eating an amulet of ESP gives you the same
type of telepathy as eating a floating eye, and with floating eyes so
common, it's not worth it.

The other intrinsics you'll need -- fire, cold, shock, sleep -- can be
gotten from corpses. Sleep can be gotten from eating elves. If the
monster uses fire or cold, eating it will give you the resistance,
generally speaking. Gelatinous cubes, some molds and some puddings
also confer resistances. Disintegration resistance can only be gained
from a black dragon corpse, but reflection handles that nicely, so
it's not absolutely necessary. You'll get these once you're crowed as
well. Shock is difficult, so I often don't get that until crowning.
You also wear the appropriate rings to fill gaps until then.

You'll also need levitation to pass over water, but not until you
reach Medusa's isle, which usually about halfway down. A ring of
levitation is easiest, because you won't have to remove your speed
boots,  but water walking boots or boots of levitation also work. Make
sure they're not cursed. In the astral plane, free action is pretty
much a must, and that can only be gotten by wearing a ring -- eating
it doesn't do the trick. A ring of conflict is also viewed as a
necessity for the endgame (and it's nice to have in the Castle as
well), but you don't want to eat it. It increases your hunger by quite
a bit.

See invisible is easily gotten from a blessed potion of see invisible.
You can also eat an invisible stalker. Permanent invisibility is also
great, but make sure you have a mummy wrapping to wear if you want to
enter shops. You can get this from a blessed potion of invisibility,
zapping yourself with a wand of make invisible or eating a stalker
corpse while invisible.

Basically, you shouldn't need to eat rings. The only ones I try to eat
are rings of protection, increase accuracy and increase damage. But
those are nice-to-haves, not must haves.






